---
arrangement: cli-help-page
owns:
  - cli-help-usage
  - cli-help-modes-heading
  - cli-help-mode-bundle
  - cli-help-mode-probe
  - cli-help-mode-plan
  - cli-help-mode-apply
  - cli-help-mode-why
  - cli-help-mode-strip
  - cli-help-mode-lint
  - cli-help-mode-none
  - cli-help-options-heading
  - cli-help-option-book
  - cli-help-option-pre-source
  - cli-help-option-oracle-dir
  - cli-help-option-results
  - cli-help-option-risk-faultless-skips
  - cli-help-option-all
  - cli-help-option-debug-argv
  - cli-help-option-help
  - cli-help-option-version
  - cli-help-receipts-heading
  - cli-help-receipt-holds
  - cli-help-receipt-kept
  - cli-help-stdin
  - cli-help-exit-codes-heading
  - cli-help-exit-success
  - cli-help-exit-usage
  - cli-help-exit-parse
  - cli-help-lint-exit-codes-heading
  - cli-help-lint-exit-clean
  - cli-help-lint-exit-findings
  - cli-help-lint-exit-operational
when-used: `dorc --help` / `dorc -h` prints this whole page to stdout and exits 0.
why: 288 rul-help-text-is-loomable: help is product prose, so it gets an editable face like every other user-facing string.
---
-- replay --
$ dorc --help
dorc: a specification-mining shell-script analyzer, compiler, and orchestrator

Hi. I analyze and run your plain-POSIX shell-scripts. Where possible, I help you
compile-in runtime idempotence (making sure your commands only run if they need
to), and even elide converged ones (removing commands entirely when they don't
actually need to be run.)

usage: dorc [<mode>] --book=<book.sh> [--pre-source <sh>]... [options]

modes (an optional leading token; default is the probe-then-apply round-trip):

bundle        print loaded dorc-lang sources as a deterministic inert archive;
              contacts no host
probe         emit only the read-only probe artifact (phase 1) to stdout; reads
              no stdin
plan          preview the eliding apply on stdout, with the why-lens +
              diagnostics on stderr
apply         emit the byte-floored, receipt-free shippable apply artifact to
              stdout
why [<addr>]  report (to stdout) WHY the run decided as it did -- bare: the
              run's problems; `book.sh:N`: the site on that source line; free
              text: matching commands
strip <file>  print <file> with every dorc dialect construct erased -- runnable
              stock POSIX sh (the off-ramp cleaner; an unmarked file passes
              through unchanged)
lint <files>  the oracle-author doctor/lint grab-bag over the files (no hosts,
              no probes): parse/cfg diagnostics, unmodeled-wall inventory,
              verdict-body checks, and shellcheck/checkbashisms when present.
              Flags: --format=human|jsonl, --fail-on=error|warn|never,
              --no-tools, --require-tools, --expect-files N, --source NAME
              (repeatable), --list-sources. Recommended CI line: `dorc lint
              --format=jsonl --fail-on=warn --require-tools --expect-files N
              <files>`
(none)        the round-trip: probe then apply on stdout, full disclosure on
              stderr

options:

<book.sh>               the one main book to analyze -- a positional path (`dorc
                        plan book.sh`) or --book=PATH / --book PATH; a second
                        one is a separate program
--pre-source <sh>       a file to dot-source before the book, in CLI order
                        (repeatable)
--oracle-dir <dir>      pre-source every *.oracle.sh in <dir> (repeatable;
                        glob-sorted)
--results <file>        read the probe results from <file>; for `why`, answer
                        from those records rather than the saved receipt
--risk-faultless-skips  opt into the survival tier (default off)
--receipts <folder>     [unwritten: cli-help-option-receipts]
--no-receipt            [unwritten: cli-help-option-no-receipt]
--receipt-last          [unwritten: cli-help-option-receipt-last]
--receipt-id <id>       [unwritten: cli-help-option-receipt-id]
--receipt <file>        [unwritten: cli-help-option-receipt]
--all                   for `why`, print everything the engine holds: every
                        link, unselected, plus collapse classes with no
                        narration yet
--json                  [unwritten: cli-help-option-json]
--debug-argv            echo the engine's per-site resolved argv to stderr
--help                  print this help to stdout and exit 0
--version               print the version to stdout and exit 0

receipts -- every plan/apply/round-trip writes one; `dorc why` reads it:

sm Holds:  book path + digest, oracles, flags, probe records, per-line sm
           disposition -- never book text, never a command's output.
sm Kept    in $XDG_STATE_HOME/dorc (else ~/.local/state/dorc; Windows sm
           %LOCALAPPDATA%\dorc). Sensitive: it names your hosts, sm paths and
           oracles: unix writes it 0600 in a 0700 directory; Windows has sm no
           mode, so there that per-user location is the *only protection.*

stdin: probe results, one per line -- `site <leafid>
effect=<holds|absent|cant-tell> rc=<n>` (only with --results -); stdout: the
selected mode's artifact(s); stderr: diagnostics / why-lens / decision-digest.

exit codes:

0   success -- the analysis completed and the artifact was emitted
2   usage error -- a bad/unknown argument, a missing --book, or an unreadable
    file
10  parse error -- the book carries a construct dorc cannot model; the artifact
    still ships byte-identically, but the exit signals partial understanding so
    a `dorc ... && deploy` chain stops. First of the reserved 10..19
    dorc-semantic fast-fail range (vacuous/obvious, dorc-specific).

lint exit codes (distinct family -- a construct dorc cannot model is a FINDING
here, never an exit-10):

0  clean -- no findings at or above --fail-on
1  findings at or above --fail-on were reported
3  operational -- the lint itself is compromised (no lintable files, an
   --expect-files mismatch, or a --require-tools absence): distinct from both
   clean and findings
